<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/expert/systems-biology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Systems biology is biology's answer to reductionism's limits. Having listed the parts, it
    asks: <strong>what do the parts do together that none does alone?</strong> It measures cells
    globally (all genes, all proteins, all metabolites at once), builds computational models of
    the networks, and tests the models by perturbing the system — engineering's loop, pointed at
    life's complexity.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the systems view, in four moves" widget="equationStep" config={sysbioCfg} />

  <h2>Why the whole is more than its parts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Robustness by design:</strong> cells survive huge perturbations — backup pathways, feedback control, redundancy. Systems models reveal the backups; single-gene thinking never saw them.</li>
    <li><strong>The whole-cell model:</strong> in 2012 Stanford built a computer model of an entire bacterial cell (every known gene and reaction, ~1,900 parameters). It predicted behaviours its builders didn't design in — the field's proof of concept.</li>
    <li><strong>Circadian clocks as case study:</strong> ~20 genes with interlocking feedback generate a self-sustaining 24-hour oscillator in every cell — a network property that survives component swaps. Nearly every organism runs one.</li>
    <li><strong>Data integration is the bottleneck:</strong> genomics, proteomics, metabolomics each measure a layer; the field's hardest problem is stitching layers into one causal model — where the AI ladder's methods and this one meet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Karr et al., Cell (2012) whole-cell model; Kitano, Science (2002); Alon, An Introduction to Systems Biology.</p>
</TopicPage>
